    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, at Tell Hariri in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, controlled river traffic on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> for over a thousand years.</li><li>In the 24th century BCE it rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>; in the 18th century it was ruled by the Amorite kings of the Lim dynasty.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi of Babylon</a> conquered it c. 1761 BCE, but its archive of some 20,000 tablets survived in the ruins.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> boasted of building an ice house at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a>, a feat he said no earlier king had achieved, to keep ice for cooling drinks at court.</p></aside>
    </section>

