    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-kamal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-kamal.jpg" alt="Abu Kamal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-kamal">Abu Kamal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Salihiyah-Al-Bukamal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agagasoft, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> stood at Tell Hariri, near the modern town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-kamal">Abu Kamal</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, on the right bank of the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> close to the present border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. Its site commanded the river route between southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and northern Syria, where the valley narrows and boats, caravans and herders all had to pass. The city was founded around 2900 BCE as a new settlement rather than growing from an older village. According to its excavator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-claude-margueron">Jean-Claude Margueron</a>, this first city was laid out on a circular plan about 1.9 kilometres across, protected by a dyke against floods and an inner rampart, and linked to the Euphrates by a canal; it worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and other metals brought down the river. After this first city declined, a second city flourished from about 2550 to 2300 BCE, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-dynastic-period">Early Dynastic period</a>, with a royal palace and temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inanna">Ishtar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> and other gods. Its kings, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iblul-il">Iblul-Il</a>, used Sumerian writing and styles, yet ruled a Semitic-speaking population and competed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> for control of the trade routes of Syria. The city’s ancient name had long been known from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list">Sumerian King List</a>, which counts a dynasty of Mari among the holders of kingship, but its site was found only in 1933.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/statue-of-ebih-il"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statue-of-ebih-il.jpg" alt="Statue of Ebih-Il" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/statue-of-ebih-il">Statue of Ebih-Il</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ebih-ii_en_priere.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Applejuice, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>’s wealth came from its position on the river. It levied tolls on boats carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, wine, oil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and in the early second millennium it was a key relay in the trade in tin from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to the west, which bronze-making cities needed. Irrigation canals on the narrow floodplain supported barley and other crops, but farming was limited, and the steppe on either side of the valley was the home of pastoral tribes with large flocks of sheep and goats. Relations between the city and these tribes, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simalites">Sim’alites</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaminites">Yaminites</a>, dominate the royal letters; the king of Mari styled himself king of Mari and of the land of Hana, a term for the tent-dwelling pastoralists. The Old Babylonian archive of about 20,000 tablets, written in Akkadian, records the running of the palace in great detail: rations, textiles, metalwork, banquets, the household of women and musicians, imports of luxury goods and gifts between courts. It also preserves letters in which prophets and ecstatics relayed messages from gods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a> and Addu to the king, and reports by diviners who read the future in the livers of sacrificed sheep. In the third millennium, statues of worshippers from Mari’s temples, such as the official <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statue-of-ebih-il">Ebih-Il</a>, show the city’s wealth and artistry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad.jpg" alt="Sargon of Akkad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sargon_of_Akkad_on_his_victory_stele.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ALFGRN, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>’s history was shaped by war with its neighbours. In the twenty-fourth century BCE its kings, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iblul-il">Iblul-Il</a>, campaigned up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> into territory controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a>, which for a time paid Mari large amounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and gold; texts from Ebla record that Ebla, allied with Nagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, later defeated Mari near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a>. The second city was destroyed around 2300 BCE, probably by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a>, who claimed to have been given Mari by the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagan">Dagan</a>, or by his successors. In the early second millennium <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahdun-lim">Yahdun-Lim</a> of the Lim dynasty fought the tribes of the steppe and neighbouring kings, and led an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, where he claimed to have washed his weapons in the sea. Soon after his death Mari fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-upper-mesopotamia">Kingdom of Upper Mesopotamia</a>, who placed his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasmah-addu">Yasmah-Addu</a> on the throne. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> regained Mari around 1775 BCE with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim of Yamhad</a> and fought to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> plains and the tribes. His troops served with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi of Babylon</a> against the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamites">Elamites</a> and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a>. Mari’s armies often numbered a few thousand men, combining royal troops, tribal contingents and allies, and its letters give rare detail on recruitment, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>, spies and the movement of armies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zimri-lim.jpg" alt="Zimri-Lim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zimrilim.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jolle, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> archive offers the fullest picture of how an Amorite kingdom of the early second millennium was governed. The king ruled from a vast palace of more than 260 rooms covering about 2.5 hectares, which served as residence, administrative centre, storehouse and workshop. He relied on governors of districts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a> and Saggaratum, who reported to him constantly by letter, and on tribal chiefs and assemblies whose consent he needed to raise troops and keep the peace; historians have used this evidence to study collective decision-making in towns and tribes alongside the monarchy. Royal women held real authority: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>’s queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shibtu">Shibtu</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim of Yamhad</a>, managed the palace and reported on affairs while he was on campaign, and his daughters were married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> region to bind them to Mari. Between the Akkadian period and the Lim dynasty Mari had been ruled by military governors called <em>shakkanakku</em>, at first appointed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a> but later hereditary rulers in their own right. In foreign affairs Mari was part of a network of competing kingdoms that exchanged envoys, letters, gifts and treaties sealed by oaths to the gods. A letter in the archive observes that no king was strong on his own: ten or fifteen kings followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>, and twenty followed Yarim-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lim-river">Lim</a> of Yamhad.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible.jpg" alt="Hebrew Bible" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tanach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LGLou, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>’s main legacy is its archive. The letters and administrative texts found in the palace from 1933 onwards by the French mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-parrot">André Parrot</a>, published in the series <em>Archives royales de Mari</em>, give historians a view of politics, diplomacy, warfare, religion and daily life in the age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> that no other site matches. They allow the reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamshi-adad-i">Shamshi-Adad</a>, Hammurabi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rim-sin-i">Rim-Sin of Larsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim of Yamhad</a> to be followed almost month by month, and they record the international system of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>, in which kings addressed each other as father, son or brother according to their rank. The prophetic letters are among the earliest evidence for prophecy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and are often compared with the prophets of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, while the evidence for pastoral tribes has informed the study of Amorite society and early Israel. Mari’s art also has lasting importance: the third-millennium statues of worshippers, the wall paintings of the palace, including the scene known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/investiture-of-zimri-lim">Investiture of Zimri-Lim</a>, and the palace’s architecture are central to the study of Syrian and Mesopotamian culture. Since 2011 the site has suffered heavy looting during the Syrian civil war.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammurabi.jpg" alt="Hammurabi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F0182_Louvre_Code_Hammourabi_Bas-relief_Sb8_rwk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mbzt, CC BY 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>’s end came through the ambitions of a former ally. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>’s kingdom flourished in the 1770s and 1760s, when he visited Ugarit with a large retinue, built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terqa">Terqa</a> an ice house that he claimed no earlier king had built, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi of Babylon</a> in resisting the Elamite invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> around 1765 BCE. After Hammurabi had defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshnunna">Eshnunna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> with the help of Mari’s troops, relations between the two kings broke down, perhaps over control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> trade and the towns between their kingdoms. Hammurabi defeated Zimri-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lim-river">Lim</a>, and Mari submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> around 1761 BCE; Zimri-Lim disappears from the record. Two years later, around 1759 BCE, Babylonian forces destroyed the city’s walls and burned the palace, after scribes had sorted and removed some of the archives. Mari never recovered as a capital. A small settlement remained on the site in later periods, but power on the middle Euphrates passed to the Kingdom of Hana, centred at Terqa upstream. The palace buried under its own ruins was preserved, with its walls standing several metres high, until a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> found a statue on the tell in 1933 and set off the excavations that restored Mari to history.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> controlled the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> for over a thousand years, and the archive of some 20,000 tablets from its last great king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, offers the most detailed view of diplomacy, war, prophecy and palace life in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> during the age of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>.</em></p>

